             Enumerating what a machine has is not the same as enumerating what could exist, but
             it is a real population where a list written from memory is a guess about one.},
  check   = {cmd:.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest tests/test_structural_query.py -k test_a_reader_or_editor_found_on_the_host_is_refused}
}

@misc{hooks-structural-query-a-non-reader-on-the-host-is-permitted,
  section = {structural-query},
  title   = {The roster encodes reads-a-claimed-artifact, not processes-text},
  note    = {pandoc is mdstruct's own backend and blocking it blocks the prescribed route; tee
             and xargs consume stdin and never open the named path. Getting the distinction wrong
             costs something in both directions and they are not symmetric: an omission fails to
             catch one command, an over-inclusion refuses one that was never the problem. A
             denylist entry firing on a non-reader is the allowlist's failure mode arriving by
             the back door.},
  check   = {cmd:.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest tests/test_structural_query.py -k test_a_non_reader_on_the_host_is_permitted}
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-the-witness-refuses-a-victim-carrying-probe-residue,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title   = {A killed witness leaves its probe, and trap EXIT cannot fire on SIGKILL},
  note    = {Measured: killing a witness inside its mutation window leaves the payload in the
             victim, and the next gate run censuses it -- reporting ratchet keys from a probe
             nobody wrote, in a file the author did not touch. SIGTERM is fine because the trap
             runs; SIGKILL is not, and no handler can make it be. The message is asserted rather
             than the code alone, because an untracked victim exits 2 as well and a test
             asserting a code two guards share proves nothing about either.},
  check   = {cmd:.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_the_witness_refuses_a_victim_carrying_probe_residue}
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-the-gate-reads-the-index-not-the-working-tree,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title   = {A property nobody designed for is one nobody is maintaining},
  note    = {The hook materialises the index and runs its checks there, so what is checked is what
             is being committed. That block predates every use made of it -- written because git
             add is usually partial -- and its most load-bearing consequence was never reasoned
             about: a second session's dirty file is invisible to this gate. A peer filed "a
             shared working tree has no safe operation" as structural, and this is why half of it
             dissolved. It had no test, so a refactor for tidiness would take the guarantee with
             it and nothing would fail. The arm is the DIFFERENCE rather than the presence:
             asserting the staged copy exists proves nothing, since it would exist under any
             implementation. F-armed by copying the working tree instead of the index, which
             carries the marker and fails the test.},
  check   = {cmd:.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_the_gate_reads_the_index_not_the_working_tree}
}

