  year   = {2026},
  section = {bar-fires},
  note   = {The poll reported the apex slot unfilled while the roster read mtools. It keyed on a
            phrase that survives in every retrospective description of the state it names,
            including the revision row recording that the slot was filled, which quotes the old
            wording to say it changed --- so the slot could not be observed as filled while its
            own revision row existed. That is the same lesson the row-filter carries in its own
            docstring, which exists because a fix and its announcement necessarily discuss the
            thing being fixed, so a table used as an instrument accretes mentions of its own
            trigger; the poll had that lesson available and did not apply it. Reported by
            rosettapkg, who touched neither file and supplied the discriminator from their own
            tree: key on position rather than wording, because a roster row's identity is its
            place in a table. The probe now reads the row by column prefix, and the verifying
            classifier written to check the report repeated the poll's own error by labelling a
            revision row as a roster row because the line contained the prefix.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_the_apex_probe_reads_a_column_rather_than_grepping_a_phrase},
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-asserted-comparisons-print-both-operands,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title  = {A gate that asserts an expression must print that expression's components},
  claim  = {The poll's roster arm asserted that the count of accounted surveyors was at least the
            gap between the roster and the legs in HEAD, and printed only the first of those two
            numbers. A reader therefore received a verdict with one operand invisible and no way
            to check the comparison that produced it. That is how the arm's population error
            survived: the count was of lines carrying a substring over a text range that included
            every paragraph explaining the mark, so it read thirteen against a roster of eight and
            then eleven after unrelated prose edits, and both figures passed the comparison. A
            count that moves when prose is edited is not counting surveyors, and one printed
            operand could not reveal that while the other stayed hidden. Printing every component
            of an asserted expression makes the line a proof-carrying artifact, or a
            disproof-carrying one, which is the half that earns it: a reader can falsify the claim
            from the line alone without re-deriving anything or trusting the deriver, where an
            asserted verdict with hidden operands can only be believed or doubted. The arm asserts
            the shape rather than the single call site, so a future branch added with a hidden
            operand fails at the point of writing rather than three ticks later when its figure
            happens to be absurd on its face.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_asserted_comparisons_print_both_operands},
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-roster-mark-is-read-from-a-cell-not-a-substring,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title  = {Count the mark in a table cell, never the string in a section},
  claim  = {The roster arm counted the filed-elsewhere mark with a case-insensitive line count over
            a stream-edited range and reported the result as a number of surveyors. It counted
            neither surveyors nor rows but lines carrying a substring, over a range that included
            the prose explaining the mark, so the vfs census read eleven against a roster of eight
            while that file's own filing-status section documented walking into the same trap twice
            in the course of writing the paragraph about it. A table used as an instrument accretes
            mentions of its own trigger, which the markdown table reader records at its own site and
            carries in the row filter's docstring: a substring filter asks whether any cell mentions
            a term, a prefix filter asks whether one column declares it. The right instrument
            existed and its documentation was written about this exact failure. Four parties reached
            the same repair independently from different corpora, one of them a conduit census that
            scored ten of nineteen rows on prose in comments and string literals and repaired it by
            reaching the read through a call node rather than a text match. The prefix predicate
            also discovered what the substring could not: the mark's wording is not uniform across
            censuses, one phrasing placing it inside a parenthetical, and a substring search cannot
            discover that its term has dialects because every dialect satisfies it. The poll now
            reports that case as unadjudicated rather than absorbing it, because a reader that
            cannot settle a question must say so rather than report a finding about itself.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_roster_mark_is_read_from_a_cell_not_a_substring},
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-roster-gap-compared-against-every-state,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title  = {A gap is not made of one state},
  claim  = {The poll's roster arm computed the gap as every rostered party lacking a leg in the
            census's own directory, then compared that gap against the count of parties marked as
            having filed elsewhere. Those are different populations. The gap includes parties that
            have not filed anywhere: a surveyor who accepted and has not yet written is pending, and
            one who declined with a measured reason is terminal, and neither is a dropped row.
            Measured on the paperkit-use census: roster eight, legs in the directory zero, gap
            eight, and the states present were four filed elsewhere, two accepted, one scoped
            decline and one not yet filed, summing to exactly eight. Every rostered party carried a
            state explaining its absence and the arm reported the one shape it exists to catch. The
            precedent for the repair is the same arm's own unadjudicated branch one axis over:
            calling a census this reader cannot parse a dropped row reports the reader, and calling
            a party that has not filed anywhere a dropped row reports the calendar. The states
            cannot be probed naively because the mark's own spelling is a prefix of another state's,
            so a summed probe double-counts; measured, a prefix query for the shorter spelling
            returns fourteen against a twelve-row table. Reported by a peer who cross-tabulated
            every census in this tree that rosters them against what their own repository holds,
            found one disagreeing row out of seven adjudicable, and handed over the row rather than
            a claim about the table, without touching the instrument.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_the_roster_gap_is_compared_against_every_state_not_only_the_mark},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-every-gate-bypass-shape-is-refused,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {Each refused shape is a route around the gate, not a variant of one string},
  claim  = {The operator's policy is never to bypass the pre-commit gate, and the shapes that bypass it
            are not spellings of a single flag. The long flag names it; its short form reaches the
            same code path; git accepts any unambiguous prefix of a long option, so shorter
            abbreviations reach it too; and pointing the hooks path at an empty or absent directory
            disarms every hook at once without naming the flag at all. A guard enumerating only the
            flag would leave the configuration route open, which is the same defect as a checker
            whose population is a hand-written list rather than a predicate. The abbreviation case
            is handled as a property, is this token a prefix of the long option long enough to be
            unambiguous, rather than as membership in a set of three spellings that would pass the
            fourth.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_every_gate_bypass_shape_is_refused},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-ordinary-commands-are-not-refused,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {The arm that makes the deny arm mean something},
  claim  = {A hook that refused every command would satisfy every deny arm in its own suite and be
            useless, so the allow arm is what gives the deny arm content. The commands held here
            include this policy's own wording quoted inside a commit message, a search for the flag
            name in a file, and a git subcommand the policy does not cover. Each is a shape a
            substring matcher refuses and git would have run, and a guard whose first act is to
            break a working session trains its owner to disable it. The false positive surface is
            therefore part of the design rather than an afterthought, which is why the token stream
            is parsed and the flag recognised only where git would recognise it.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_ordinary_commands_are_not_refused},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-an-unparseable-command-is-not-a-bypass,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {An unparseable command is a statement about the parser},
  claim  = {A command with unbalanced quotes cannot be parsed, and the shell would refuse it for the
            same reason. Reporting a policy violation against such a string would be a statement
            about the reader wearing the shape of a finding about the command, which is the defect
            class this repository has measured repeatedly in its own checkers. The hook returns no
            findings in that case, so an unparseable string is neither approved nor accused.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_an_unparseable_command_is_not_a_bypass},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-the-installed-console-script-denies-when-armed,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {The predicate and the entry point are different claims},
  claim  = {Every unit arm exercises the matching function directly. A hook can have a correct
            predicate and a broken entry point, and only an end to end arm can tell those apart.
            This repository has the measured instance: a sibling hook shipped with a rubric section,
            fifty two tests and fifty two warrants while the settings file wired only its sibling,
            and the module docstring said so in its first paragraph while the suite stayed green. So
            this arm runs the installed console script with a real harness payload and reads the
            decision out of its output rather than trusting that the wiring resolves.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_the_installed_console_script_denies_when_armed},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-the-installed-console-script-is-silent-when-unarmed,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {An unarmed hook must emit no decision at all, not an advisory},
  claim  = {Measured by a peer repository: an unarmed hook that exits zero with no permission decision
            is read by the harness as allow, nothing to report, so any advisory text it prints
            reaches nobody. An advisory tier therefore looks like caution and behaves like silence,
            and a guard in that state reads as armed in review while detecting every violation and
            reporting none. Two states only, armed or absent. This arm holds the hook to that rather
            than letting a third state grow.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_the_installed_console_script_is_silent_when_unarmed},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-the-hook-is-wired-into-settings,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {A shipped hook nothing invokes is the packager not using its own package},
  claim  = {This repository's named defect, measured on its own sibling, which carried full coverage
            and no entry point while its own docstring recorded the fact. The wiring is therefore an
            assertion rather than a convention. The arm also requires that the arming be inline in
            the command string rather than in the environment block alone, because two peers
            measured independently that a session already running when the settings file changes
            never picks up the environment, so the hooks keep exiting zero and the guard is off in
            fact while reading as armed in review.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_the_hook_is_wired_into_settings},
}

@misc{hooks-no-verify-the-post-commit-verifies-the-gate-ran-before-pushing,
  section = {no_verify},
  title  = {Two mechanisms, and they fail differently},
  claim  = {The pre tool use hook reads a command string and can be evaded by a spelling nobody
            enumerated. The post commit hook reads the result: a commit whose tree the gate never
            verified carries no witness, however it was produced. Neither mechanism subsumes the
            other, which is why both were ruled. The witness keys on the staged tree because it must,
            since the gate runs before the commit object exists and there is no revision to name
            yet, while the commit that follows carries the same tree the gate verified. The absence
            of the witness file and a tree missing from a present witness are different facts: the
            first says the gate has never run in this clone, which is a statement about the
            environment, and the second says it ran for other commits and not this one, which is the
            bypass. Collapsing them would be this repository's standing defect one layer out, so the
            hook reports them separately and refuses to push in either case.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_no_verify.py -k test_the_post_commit_verifies_the_gate_ran_before_pushing},
}

@misc{hooks-bar-fires-partition-terms-and-total-from-different-measurements,
  section = {bar-fires},
  title  = {A partition's terms and its total must come from different measurements},
  claim  = {The rule that an asserted expression must print its components stops a hidden operand.
            It does not stop a partition whose terms all read zero because one failed match
            produced every one of them, and in that case the printed terms prove only that they
            agree with each other. Agreement among four readings of a single failure is not
            evidence. The bound was supplied by a peer against a rule they had just adopted from
            this tree, and they found it in their own instrument first: a figure line reading zero
            of at-least-zero, which is true of every population, a claim that cannot be wrong
            wearing the shape of a clean result, and whose floor notation made it look deliberate.
            Measuring the same shape here found a second defect underneath. On the build-hermeticity
            census all four state terms read zero while a prefix query for the shortest state
            returns fourteen rows at exit zero, so the reader parses that file perfectly. Every
            status row there carries a terminal state the four probes never enumerated, because the
            probes were written from the two censuses their author happened to be reading: a
            hand-written vocabulary inside the arm written to repair a hand-written population. The
            repair reads the total from the status table itself rather than summing the probes, and
            prints the remainder, so that the rows the probes could not classify are visible rather
            than absorbed into a zero. The all-zero case is reported as its own verdict ahead of the
            others because it explains them, in the same three-state discipline the unadjudicated
            branch already applies one axis over.},
  check  = {pytest hooks/tests/test_bar_fires.py -k test_a_partitions_terms_and_total_come_from_different_measurements},
}
